Here's how to implement global error handling with `IExceptionHandler`:

**1. Create `Middleware/ApiExceptionHandler.cs`:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

namespace YourApp.Middleware;

internal sealed class ApiExceptionHandler(ILogger<ApiExceptionHandler> logger)
    : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext httpContext,
        Exception exception,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Not Found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Bad Request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (0, (string?)null)
        };

        if (statusCode == 0)
            return false; // Default handler returns 500 with no sensitive details

        logger.LogWarning(exception, "Handled API exception: {Title}", title);

        httpContext.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;
        await httpContext.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = title,
            Instance = httpContext.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken);

        return true;
    }
}
```

**2. Register in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<ApiExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();

var app = builder.Build();

app.UseExceptionHandler();  // Catches unhandled exceptions → 500 ProblemDetails
app.UseStatusCodePages();   // Converts bare status codes to ProblemDetails
```

**How it works:**

- `AddProblemDetails()` configures the default exception handler to return RFC 7807 JSON for unhandled exceptions (500) with no stack trace or sensitive info.
- Your custom `IExceptionHandler` runs first — if it recognizes the exception type, it writes a ProblemDetails response and returns `true`. Otherwise it returns `false`, deferring to the built-in handler.
- No try-catch needed in endpoints — just throw naturally from your service layer.

**Example response (404):**

```json
{
  "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.5",
  "title": "Not Found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "Not Found",
  "instance": "/api/products/999"
}
```

This requires .NET 8+. The middleware pipeline order matters — place `UseExceptionHandler()` early (before routing/endpoints).
